# SQL Migration File Format

SQL Migration is an ordinary SQL file.

File name must have the following format:

    YYYYmmddHHMMSS_database.sql

'YYYYmmddHHMMSS' should be the current date in UTC.
'database' must be one of 'characters', 'logon', 'logs' or 'world'.

For example:

    20140320193109_world.sql

Use `make_migration.py <database>` (or `.sh`/`.bat`) to create one with the right template.

## characters, logon, logs

These are MySQL, applied by concatenating them in filename order and running the result through the
`mysql` client (`merge.sh`/`merge.bat`). Each file is a stored procedure:

    DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS add_migration;
    DELIMITER ??
    CREATE PROCEDURE `add_migration`()
    BEGIN
    DECLARE v INT DEFAULT 1;
    SET v = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM `migrations` WHERE `id`='YYYYmmddHHMMSS');
    IF v = 0 THEN
    INSERT INTO `migrations` VALUES ('YYYYmmddHHMMSS');
    -- Add your query below.
    END IF;
    END??
    DELIMITER ;
    CALL add_migration();
    DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS add_migration;

The `IF v=0` guard makes re-running a merged file a no-op.

## world

`world` is DoltLite (SQLite), applied by `database_tool` one file at a time, not merged. There is no
procedure envelope. First line in the file must be:

    INSERT INTO `migrations` VALUES ('YYYYmmddHHMMSS');

followed directly by the migration's statements, in SQLite dialect. `database_tool update` skips files
whose id is already recorded and wraps the rest of the file in one transaction, so a file still runs
once and runs whole even without the guard. A migration copied in from upstream vmangos MySQL dialect
is converted first with `database_tool port-migration <file>`.
